    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d355">

                <pb facs="0524" n="422" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg422"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/2106: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-01-31T18:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 31,
                                1919, 6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received 6.24
                            p.m.</hi>]</dateline></opener>
                <p>676. Your 727 January 18th, noon.<anchor n="71" xml:id="d355-f1"/> I understand
                    that the attitude of the British Government towards the completion of the
                    International Consortium now under discussion, has not in the past been
                    favorable. It had been thought here that, owing to the inclusion of industrial
                    loans in the proposed agreement, the American group would consist of industrial
                    as well as financial institutions and that <pb facs="0525" n="423"
                        xml:id="pg423"/> this would result in a monopoly of all business both
                    financial and industrial. Foreign Office did not favor joining a consortium
                    which might involve support of a monopoly which they could not justify. Morgan,
                    who I understand will take up this matter in Paris also next week, has explained
                    here that there was no thought of such monopoly but that the plan now under
                    consideration has to do with financial affairs only. This explanation seems to
                    have given satisfaction and it is hoped the matter will now receive favorable
                    consideration in Great Britain.</p>
                <p>Hukuang loan belonging to the original four powers, United States, Great Britain,
                    France and Germany, feeling here is that the transference of German interests to
                    Japan should only be offered to Japanese as part of the whole transaction and
                    should be made as a concession and part of the advantage Japan would receive
                    from coming into the consortium.<anchor n="72" xml:id="d355-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
